# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries_en> ,
              <https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1945-59_en> ,
              <https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1990-99_en> ,
              <https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/2000-09_en> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q458> ,
              <https://www.ecb.europa.eu/euro/intro/html/index.en.html> ,
              <https://eur-lex.europa.eu/EN/legal-content/summary/treaty-of-rome-eec.html> ,
              <https://economy-finance.ec.europa.eu/euro/eu-countries-and-euro/bulgaria-and-euro_en> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "The set of member states: the EU's own list of countries (query 6, european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries_en, fetched on 2026-10-04) shows \"Pages (27)\" and the year filter \"Countries by year of entry 1958 1973 1981 1986 1995 2004 2007 2013\". Wikidata gave the same 27 (query 2: items with a P463 \"member of\" statement for the European Union, Q458, of any rank, with the P580/P582 qualifiers; the United Kingdom, Q145, is the one item with an end time, 2020-01-31). A first Wikidata query (query 1) that also required P31 sovereign state (Q3624078) returned only 25 of them, because the Netherlands (Q55) and Denmark (Q35) are modelled as constituent countries of their kingdoms; the class filter was dropped."@en ,
                 "Dates: fetch_profiles.py (query 5, whose full source is recorded there) downloaded the English and Swedish country profile of each of the 27 countries (…/eu-countries/<country>_en and …_sv) and extracted the overview, which gives \"EU Member State: since <date>\" and, for euro countries, \"Euro area: member since <date>\" (in Swedish \"EU-medlem: Sedan den …\" and \"Euroområdet: Medlem sedan den …\"). The year on each back is the year of these dates; the full date is in the back note. The English and Swedish pages agree on every date. Countries whose profile names another currency (Czechia, Denmark, Hungary, Poland, Romania, Sweden) have no euro card."@en ,
                 "Conventions: for the six founders the profiles give 1 January 1958, the day the Treaties of Rome (signed 25 March 1957) came into force and the EEC and Euratom came into being (europa.eu history page 1945-59, query 8; EUR-Lex summary of the EEC Treaty, query 10). Other sources use 1957 (the ECB) or record the signing date (Wikidata), so the convention is stated in the description and in each founder's back note. For the euro, the year is when the country joined the euro area: the profiles give 1 January 1999 for the first eleven, when the euro was introduced for non-cash transactions (europa.eu history page 1990-99, query 8), and 1 January 2001 for Greece; notes and coins came on 1 January 2002 (europa.eu history page 2000-09, query 13, entry \"1 January 2002 – Euro notes and coins launch in 12 countries\"), which the back notes of those twelve cards say. From Slovenia (2007) on, the profiles, the ECB and Wikidata give one date."@en ,
                 "Names: the English names are the profiles' (\"Czechia\" rather than Wikidata's English label \"Czech Republic\", which has \"Czechia\" as an alias; \"Netherlands\"). The Swedish names are the Wikidata Swedish labels (query 4), each identical to the name the Swedish profile uses in its title and text (Österrike, Belgien, Tjeckien, Nederländerna …). The Swedish card wording follows the Swedish profiles (\"EU-medlem sedan\", \"Medlem sedan den\", \"euroområdet\"); month names are lower-case in the Swedish notes."@en ,
                 "Cross-check against the European Central Bank: the ECB's page \"Our money\" (query 7; the old address …/map.en.html redirects to …/index.en.html) has a table of the EU members with \"Joined the EU\" and \"Adopted the euro\" (\"1999 (cash since 2002)\", Greece \"2001 (cash since 2002)\", Bulgaria \"2026\"), and lists Czech Republic, Denmark, Hungary, Poland, Romania and Sweden as not using the euro. Every card agrees with it except the founders' year (ECB 1957), which follows the stated convention. Bulgaria's euro date, the most recent change, was checked a third time against the Commission's page \"Bulgaria and the euro\" (query 12)."@en ,
                 "Cross-check against Wikidata (query 2 and query 3): the P580 start time of each later member's P463 Q458 statement is the profiles' date (1973-01-01 … 2013-07-01). Wikidata gives 1957-03-25 for Belgium, France, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands and 1958-01-01 for Germany. Wikidata's euro P38 statements (Q4916, preferred rank) have start times equal to the profiles' dates except Germany, Italy and Greece, where they are 2002-01-01, the date the notes and coins replaced the national currency. These differences are recorded in each card's evidence and in quality control; the cards follow the EU's own dates."@en ,
                 "Notes were written in the compiler's own words from the dates above. make_dossier.py (query 14, its key steps recorded there) wrote the cards from the saved files (profiles.json, the Wikidata CSV results of queries 2–4 and the ECB page): it stripped the HTML tags from each page (scripts and styles removed, every tag replaced by a space, entities unescaped, runs of whitespace collapsed to one space), removed the space this leaves before a colon (regular expression \\s+: replaced by :), and took each quoted date as the exact match of the patterns \"EU Member State: since (\\d+) (\\w+) (\\d{4})\", \"EU-medlem: Sedan den (\\d+) (\\w+) (\\d{4})\", \"Euro area: member since (\\d+) (\\w+) (\\d{4})\" and \"Euroområdet: Medlem sedan den (\\d+) (\\w+) (\\d{4})\" in the profile overviews, and each ECB quote as the country's row in the ECB table. The quotes from the history pages and EUR-Lex were copied from the page text by hand, with the space the tag stripping left before a comma or full stop removed."@en ,
                 "Machine checks run by the builder against live Wikidata: for every card the English (for Czechia the alias \"Czechia\") and Swedish label of the country; for every membership card P463 = Q458, except Romania, whose Q458 statement is not a truthy value because another of its P463 statements has preferred rank; for every euro card P38 = Q4916. The builder can check only direct (truthy) values, not the P580 qualifiers, so the years were compared with Wikidata through queries 2 and 3 as described above."@en ,
                 "Selection: All 27 member states of the European Union on 2026-10-04, as the EU's own list shows them, with one card each for the year of membership, and the 21 of them that use the euro, with one card each for the year they joined the euro area. Not included: the United Kingdom, a member from 1973 until it left on 31 January 2020; the six members that do not use the euro (Czechia, Denmark, Hungary, Poland, Romania, Sweden) have no euro card; countries that use the euro without being EU members (Andorra, Monaco, San Marino, Vatican City, Montenegro, Kosovo) and candidate countries. The membership year is the year in the EU's country profiles: for the founders 1958, when the Treaties of Rome came into force, not 1952 (the European Coal and Steel Community) or 1957 (the signing) — the European Union itself was created by the Maastricht Treaty on 1 November 1993, and membership is counted, as the EU counts it, from the Communities that became the EU. Former territories and changes of borders (such as German reunification in 1990) are not covered. The euro year is the year of joining the euro area, not of the cash changeover. Facts that could change (new members, new euro countries) are as of October 2026."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC BY 4.0 because its main content source, the EU's website european-union.europa.eu (the country profiles and the three history pages for 1945-59, 1990-99 and 2000-09, each recorded as its own source), is CC BY 4.0 under the Commission's reuse policy (Decision 2011/833/EU); the deck attributes it in its metadata (prov:wasDerivedFrom, with title, creator and licence) and in the provenance report, and the cards are the compiler's own wording of the dates, which is a change from the source and is stated as such here. The other content source, Wikidata, is CC0. The verification sources contributed no text or selection: the ECB's website is copyright of the ECB with reuse allowed on its own conditions (treated as all rights reserved, verification only), and the EUR-Lex summary and the Commission's page on Bulgaria are CC BY 4.0 but only confirmed facts the content sources gave. The selection is the complete set of current EU members and of the euro-area members, not a choice taken from anyone's list. Quotations in the provenance report's evidence remain under their sources' licences and are given only to make the checks verifiable."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every date against the EU's English and Swedish country profiles, Wikidata and the ECB; the founders' convention against the EU history page and EUR-Lex; Bulgaria's euro date also against the Commission; the names against Wikidata labels and the Swedish profiles."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 48 cards (27 membership, 21 euro), the sources and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The EU's profiles (English and Swedish), the ECB and Wikidata agree on the membership year of the 21 later members and on the euro year of 18 of the 21 euro countries. The differences are conventions, not errors: the founders' year (EU profiles 1958, ECB 1957, Wikidata 1957 for five and 1958 for Germany) and the euro year of Germany, Italy and Greece in Wikidata (2002, the cash changeover). The cards follow the EU's own dates and state the conventions. Builder checks cover the English and Swedish country names of every card, P463 Q458 on 26 of 27 membership cards and P38 Q4916 on all euro cards; no check was dropped because Wikidata disagreed, but the qualifier years cannot be machine-checked by the builder."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 48 cards (27 membership, 21 euro), against the European Parliament enlargement fact sheet, the Commission's \"EU countries and the euro\" page, the ECB table and live Wikidata qualifiers."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual errors found: every membership and euro year agrees with the European Parliament, the Commission (DG ECFIN) and the ECB, the founders follow the stated 1958 convention, the six non-euro members match, and the builder's 143 Wikidata checks passed live. Two suggestions: a cut-off on Croatia's \"most recent country to join\" note (applied) and a remark that the builder's checks do not cover the years (already stated in round 0; no change)."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 48 cards, and the title, description and keywords in English and Swedish."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Swedish country names, card wording, date style and language tags (en/sv fronts and notes, zxx years, no untagged text) were found correct. One warning, applied: the Swedish cut-off \"(i oktober 2026)\" on bulgaria-euro read as the month the euro was introduced. Three suggestions, all applied: the founders' note named its date instead of \"from then\" / \"från dess\", Croatia's note got a cut-off, and the English description uses typographic quotes. The review's remark that keywords are untagged in the TTL concerns the builder, not the dossier."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 48 cards' wording and evidence, every source's licence page, the deck licence, the method and the recorded queries."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No compliance errors: every source licence was confirmed and CC BY 4.0 complies with all content sources; the ECB is rightly verification-only; the quoted profile dates, ECB rows, history quotes, EUR-Lex quote and Wikidata ranks and start times were found verbatim and correct. Three warnings, all applied: the cash date 1 January 2002 in twelve notes now cites the CC BY europa.eu history page 2000-09; the history pages are split into one source each, so all three appear in prov:wasDerivedFrom; fetch_profiles.py is recorded in full and make_dossier.py's extraction steps are recorded. One suggestion, applied: the ECB licence evidence names the address fetched and its redirect."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card changed."@en .

